=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the LU factorization of $A$ is\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nour equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ni.e.,\n\n$$\nLU\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we set\n\n$$\nU\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the above, we have\n\n$$\nL\\mathbf{y}= \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nIn other words, we can reduce the problem to the following system of matrix equations:\n\n$$\n{Ly = b; Ux = y\n$$\n\nSince $L$ and $U$ are both triangular, each of these equations is easily solvable using back and forward substitution.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}$. Let's assume that $\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ Then, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhich gives the system of equations\n${y_{1} =-1; - y_{1} + y_{2} = 0$.\nSolving this equation using *forward* substitution, we get the solution\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Finally, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}$. We have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhich gives the system of equations\n${x_{1} - 3x_{2} =-1; - x_{2} =-1$.\nSolving this equation using *back* substitution, we get the solution\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533440 17592186045585 false] [17592186121037 1064 "There is an algorithm that uses $LU$ factorization to solve systems of equations of the form \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince the LU factorization of $A$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nour equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ni.e., \