w echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** To get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n- we can only add a multiple of the $1$st row to the $2$nd row ($R_2:=R_2+k R_1$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." 13194139533440 17592186045585 true] [17592186121073 1064 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the matrices $L$ and $U$, we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + 3R_{1}⟺\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(E_{1}) \\cdot A \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= U\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular